<Layout title="Editorial approach | Clean Care"><section class="container article"><p class="eyebrow">Why Clean Care exists</p><h1>Information first.<br/>Sources in view.</h1><div class="prose"><p>Clean Care is an educational site about fragrance exposure and health. It explains where exposure can happen, what research says about possible consequences, what remains uncertain, and practical ways to avoid or reduce exposure. It is not a product brand or store.</p><h2>How articles are developed</h2><p>Community conversations can identify questions worth investigating, including topics discussed in the fragrance-sensitivity Facebook group named in the project brief. We do not present group posts as scientific proof or reproduce a member’s words without permission. Articles should trace important health claims to original research, public health agencies, or clearly identified expert presentations, and explain the limits of those sources.</p><h2>What the language means</h2><p>“Fragrance” covers many possible ingredients and mixtures. Health effects depend on the substance, exposure, and person. We describe documented concerns such as contact allergy and reported respiratory symptoms without claiming that every synthetic fragrance is toxic at normal use. A pleasant odor, a “natural” label, or no odor is not a reliable safety test.</p><h2>Products by country</h2><p>Product recommendations are a secondary resource. The directory groups products by individual country, not by continent. Profiles use country-specific brand sources, official product images, and direct non-affiliate links. Current coverage includes the US, UK, Singapore, Australia and New Zealand. Each identifies its source, date, and limitations. Manufacturer claims are not independent testing or a promise that a product will suit everyone. We will disclose any future commercial relationship beside the recommendation.</p><h2>Individual health decisions</h2><p>Information on this site is educational. If a symptom is severe or persistent, seek advice from a qualified health professional. Botanical artwork is decorative and does not imply that houseplants clean indoor air.</p></div></section></Layout>
